                    <!-- 5. Advanced Surgical Approach -->
                    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-9 mb-4 border-b border-slate-100 pb-3">Our Surgical Approach &amp; Mesh Selection</h2>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-5">
                        Hernia repairs performed by Dr. Kumaragurubaran are designed to withstand long-term mechanical stress, particularly for active individuals and physical laborers in Tamil Nadu. Core pillars of our surgical protocol include:
                    </p>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm md:text-base mb-6">
                        <li><strong>TEP (Totally Extraperitoneal) Priority:</strong> For uncomplicated groin hernias, TEP repair is preferred because it works entirely outside the abdominal cavity, placing mesh between the transversalis fascia and peritoneum without touching the intestines.</li>
                        <li><strong>TAPP (Transabdominal Preperitoneal) Versatility:</strong> Ideal for complex, irreducible, or recurrent hernias, and for patients with prior pelvic surgery or concurrent contralateral occult hernias requiring bilateral inspection.</li>
                        <li><strong>eTEP Abdominal Wall Reconstruction:</strong> For large incisional and ventral hernias, extended TEP provides panoramic visualization to release the retro-rectus space (Rives-Stoppa) and perform Transversus Abdominis Release (TAR) without leaving intraperitoneal foreign bodies.</li>
                        <li><strong>Macroporous Lightweight Mesh:</strong> We utilize modern monofilament polypropylene and 3D anatomical pre-formed meshes that integrate with surrounding collagen while minimizing chronic foreign body sensations.</li>
                        <li><strong>Nerve-Sparing Fixation:</strong> Mesh fixation uses self-gripping micro-grips, absorbable tacks, or medical tissue glue (cyanoacrylate). We avoid permanent metal tacks in critical nerve zones (such as the &ldquo;Triangle of Pain&rdquo;) to prevent chronic post-herniorrhaphy groin neuralgia.</li>
                    </ul>

